item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso :: pub fn sso( &self, aud: &str, headers: &HashMap<String, String>, query: &HashMap<String, String>, ) -> Reply { :: `GET /api/auth/sso/<aud>` — the cross-site handoff to one allowlisted audience (`mutable`, `exocortexia`, `wishnode`).  Layering follows the claims/submissions precedent in the servers: a signed-in account is enough; an editor's place is not required (`sso_admits` is the single predicate that decides). With a session, the reply 302s to the audience's callback with a freshly sealed assertion. Without one, it 302s to this site's own root with this endpoint as the `next`, so the ordinary provider sign-in returns the browser here and the handoff completes — the OAuth `next` itself stays same-site throughout. An audience outside the allowlist, or one not configured (no SSO secret, or no target for that audience), simply does not exist: 404 — checked before any session handling, so an unknown audience never enters the sign-in return flow. :: doc comment :: 1340 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_assertion :: pub fn sso_assertion( &self, aud: &str, session: &Session, editor: bool, ret: &str, ) -> Result<String, String> { :: Mint the assertion an audience's callback verifies: who this session is, whether it is an editor, and the return path on the audience's site — sealed under the shared SSO secret, alive for `SSO_TTL` seconds, with a fresh `jti` the audience burns on first sight. The format is identical for every audience; only the `aud` claim differs, and an audience outside the allowlist in `sso_target_for` can never be minted for. The return path is clamped by `safe_next` here, at the moment of sealing, so no unsigned return input ever crosses. :: doc comment :: 618 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_ready :: pub fn sso_ready(&self) -> bool { :: True when the Mutable SSO handoff is configured: a real SSO secret and a valid Mutable target. Independent of `ready()` — the handoff mints nothing of its own; it rides the session the providers already made, so it needs no provider keys here. :: doc comment :: 385 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_ready_for :: pub fn sso_ready_for(&self, aud: &str) -> bool { :: True when the SSO handoff is configured for one audience: the shared SSO secret is real and that audience has a target. Every allowlisted audience rides the same secret and the same sealed assertion format; only the `aud` claim and the callback origin differ. :: doc comment :: 394 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_target :: pub fn sso_target(&self) -> &str { :: Function sso_target takes no inputs beyond the value it is called on, and gives back &str. :: signature :: 402 :: 0
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_target_for :: pub fn sso_target_for(&self, aud: &str) -> Option<&str> { :: The callback origin for an audience, or None when the audience is not allowlisted or is allowlisted but unconfigured. Mutable always has a target (its production origin is the default); Exocortexia has one only once `PHEXT_SSO_EXOCORTEXIA_URL` is set, and Wishnode only once `PHEXT_SSO_WISHNODE_URL` is. This match is the audience allowlist — an audience that is not named here can never be minted for. :: doc comment :: 413 :: 1
