item+ function src/oauth.rs :: loopback_return :: pub fn loopback_return(raw: &str) -> Option<String> { :: Admit a desktop return URL: the address of the listener the app opened for this one sign-in attempt. The bar is the loopback literal itself, checked character by character — not a URL parse that a hostname or userinfo could slip through:  * scheme `http` exactly. Loopback has a real origin but no CA issues certificates for it; RFC 8252's native-app practice is plain http on the loopback interface, which never leaves the machine. * host the literal `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]` — not `localhost`, not any other name, not another loopback address. A name is whatever the resolver says it is (hosts files are editable), and the app binds the numeric loopback anyway. * an explicit port, 1–65535 — the app always listens on an ephemeral port it was given, never a default. * then only a path/query tail of plain URL characters: no `#` (a fragment never reaches a server anyway, and its presence means the value was not a listener address), no whitespace, controls, or non-ASCII.  The result is capped short enough to ride the sign-in bounce — this endpoint travels as the `next` of a `/?next=` redirect, and `safe_next` refuses anything over 200 characters there. :: doc comment :: 1793 :: 1
