item+ struct src/oauth.rs :: Session :: pub struct Session { :: A data shape named Session. Its fields are: pub who: String; pub handle: String; pub name: String; pub email: String; pub csrf: String. :: signature :: 218 :: 0
item+ struct src/oauth.rs :: SsoAssertionBody :: pub struct SsoAssertionBody { :: The SSO assertion: a sealed, single-use statement that an edit session exists, carried to an audience's callback in a redirect and verified there under the SSO secret. It is not a session — the audience mints its own from it, and this server never accepts one back. `aud` names the one audience it was minted for (`mutable`, `exocortexia`, `wishnode`); a consumer must reject any other value, including an assertion minted for a different audience. `ret` is a path on the audience's site, clamped by `safe_next` before sealing; `jti` is the single-use id the audience burns on first sight.  The one exception to "never accepts one back" is the `desktop` audience (Phext Notepad): a shipped app cannot hold the SSO secret, so its assertions come back here to `POST /api/auth/desktop/exchange`, which verifies them under the secret and burns the `jti` server-side. For that audience `ret` is the app's validated loopback return URL (see `loopback_return`), and `st` carries the app's per-attempt state nonce, sealed at mint and demanded back at the exchange. Web audiences never carry `st` — the field is simply absent from their assertions. :: doc comment :: 269 :: 1
