item+ function src/oauth.rs :: safe_next :: pub fn safe_next(next: &str) -> String { :: Keeps the post-sign-in redirect on this site: a path, optionally with a `#coordinate` fragment. :: doc comment :: 1757 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: session :: pub fn session(&self, headers: &HashMap<String, String>) -> Option<Session> { :: The signed-in session for a request, if any. In `--local` mode this is the synthetic local developer (never for a forwarded request), mirroring `session_view`; otherwise it is the cookie session. :: doc comment :: 1225 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso :: pub fn sso( &self, aud: &str, headers: &HashMap<String, String>, query: &HashMap<String, String>, ) -> Reply { :: `GET /api/auth/sso/<aud>` — the cross-site handoff to one allowlisted audience (`mutable`, `exocortexia`, `wishnode`).  Layering follows the claims/submissions precedent in the servers: a signed-in account is enough; an editor's place is not required (`sso_admits` is the single predicate that decides). With a session, the reply 302s to the audience's callback with a freshly sealed assertion. Without one, it 302s to this site's own root with this endpoint as the `next`, so the ordinary provider sign-in returns the browser here and the handoff completes — the OAuth `next` itself stays same-site throughout. An audience outside the allowlist, or one not configured (no SSO secret, or no target for that audience), simply does not exist: 404 — checked before any session handling, so an unknown audience never enters the sign-in return flow. :: doc comment :: 1463 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: token_eq :: pub fn token_eq(expected: &str, given: &str) -> bool { :: Constant-time comparison; an empty expected value never matches. :: doc comment :: 1922 :: 1
