item+ function src/oauth.rs :: loopback_return :: pub fn loopback_return(raw: &str) -> Option<String> { :: Admit a desktop return URL: the address of the listener the app opened for this one sign-in attempt. The bar is the loopback literal itself, checked character by character — not a URL parse that a hostname or userinfo could slip through:  * scheme `http` exactly. Loopback has a real origin but no CA issues certificates for it; RFC 8252's native-app practice is plain http on the loopback interface, which never leaves the machine. * host the literal `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]` — not `localhost`, not any other name, not another loopback address. A name is whatever the resolver says it is (hosts files are editable), and the app binds the numeric loopback anyway. * an explicit port, 1–65535 — the app always listens on an ephemeral port it was given, never a default. * then only a path/query tail of plain URL characters: no `#` (a fragment never reaches a server anyway, and its presence means the value was not a listener address), no whitespace, controls, or non-ASCII.  The result is capped short enough to ride the sign-in bounce — this endpoint travels as the `next` of a `/?next=` redirect, and `safe_next` refuses anything over 200 characters there. :: doc comment :: 1820 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: matches :: pub fn matches(&self, who: &str, email: &str) -> bool { :: Function matches takes &self, who: &str, email: &str and gives back bool. :: signature :: 895 :: 0
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: mcp_oauth_hash :: pub fn mcp_oauth_hash(&self, domain: &str, value: &str) -> Option<String> { :: A domain-separated, keyed digest for the MCP OAuth 2.1 store.  Authorization codes, access tokens, refresh tokens, and client secrets are never stored in the clear. Each value is represented by HMAC-SHA256 under the existing session secret, with the caller's domain label mixed in before the value, so a digest minted for one kind of credential cannot be replayed as another. No separate OAuth secret is required: rotating the session secret intentionally invalidates the stored OAuth credentials along with sessions. :: doc comment :: 634 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: oauth :: pub fn oauth(&self) -> &Oauth { :: Function oauth takes no inputs beyond the value it is called on, and gives back &Oauth. :: signature :: 1240 :: 0
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: open_sso_assertion :: pub fn open_sso_assertion(&self, token: &str) -> Result<SsoAssertionBody, String> { :: Open an SSO assertion under the SSO secret — the exchange endpoint is the one place this server ever takes an assertion back, and only the desktop audience's. Expiry is checked here; audience, state, and single-use are the caller's to enforce (the burn lives on the `Gate`, which owns request state). Every failure is the same error: the exchange must not say which check a forged assertion failed. :: doc comment :: 778 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: parse :: pub fn parse(raw: &str) -> Self { :: Function parse takes raw: &str and gives back Self. :: signature :: 880 :: 0
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: route :: pub fn route(&self, method: &str, path: &str, headers: &HashMap<String, String>) -> Option<Reply> { :: Handles `/api/session` and `/api/auth/*`. Returns None for other paths. :: doc comment :: 1312 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: safe_next :: pub fn safe_next(next: &str) -> String { :: Keeps the post-sign-in redirect on this site: a path, optionally with a `#coordinate` fragment. :: doc comment :: 1783 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: security_header_block :: pub fn security_header_block(https: bool) -> String { :: Function security_header_block takes https: bool and gives back String. :: signature :: 1114 :: 0
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: session :: pub fn session(&self, headers: &HashMap<String, String>) -> Option<Session> { :: The signed-in session for a request, if any. In `--local` mode this is the synthetic local developer (never for a forwarded request), mirroring `session_view`; otherwise it is the cookie session. :: doc comment :: 1251 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso :: pub fn sso( &self, aud: &str, headers: &HashMap<String, String>, query: &HashMap<String, String>, ) -> Reply { :: `GET /api/auth/sso/<aud>` — the cross-site handoff to one allowlisted audience (`mutable`, `exocortexia`, `wishnode`).  Layering follows the claims/submissions precedent in the servers: a signed-in account is enough; an editor's place is not required (`sso_admits` is the single predicate that decides). With a session, the reply 302s to the audience's callback with a freshly sealed assertion. Without one, it 302s to this site's own root with this endpoint as the `next`, so the ordinary provider sign-in returns the browser here and the handoff completes — the OAuth `next` itself stays same-site throughout. An audience outside the allowlist, or one not configured (no SSO secret, or no target for that audience), simply does not exist: 404 — checked before any session handling, so an unknown audience never enters the sign-in return flow. :: doc comment :: 1489 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_assertion :: pub fn sso_assertion( &self, aud: &str, session: &Session, editor: bool, ret: &str, ) -> Result<String, String> { :: Mint the assertion an audience's callback verifies: who this session is, whether it is an editor, and the return path on the audience's site — sealed under the shared SSO secret, alive for `SSO_TTL` seconds, with a fresh `jti` the audience burns on first sight. The format is identical for every audience; only the `aud` claim differs, and an audience outside the allowlist in `sso_target_for` can never be minted for. The return path is clamped by `safe_next` here, at the moment of sealing, so no unsigned return input ever crosses. :: doc comment :: 701 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_assertion_desktop :: pub fn sso_assertion_desktop( &self, session: &Session, editor: bool, ret: &str, state: &str, ) -> Result<String, String> { :: Mint the desktop audience's assertion. Same sealed format as every audience, with the two differences that audience exists for: `ret` is the app's loopback return URL, validated by `loopback_return` here at the moment of sealing (anything else is refused, so no unvalidated return ever crosses), and the app's per-attempt `state` nonce rides sealed as `st`, to be demanded back at the exchange — an assertion that arrives anywhere without its attempt's state is not that attempt's assertion. The caller (`Gate::sso_desktop`) has already validated the state's shape. :: doc comment :: 740 :: 1
