item+ function src/oauth.rs :: clean_twitter_handle :: pub fn clean_twitter_handle(raw: &str) -> String { :: An X username verbatim: `[A-Za-z0-9_]{1,15}`, case preserved. Unlike `clean_handle` this keeps underscores — they are load-bearing in X handles (@Grady_Booch, @4ster_light) and the wavefront lookup keys on them. Anything outside the charset makes the handle unusable (""). :: doc comment :: 1948 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: display_name :: pub fn display_name(raw: &str, fallback: &str) -> String { :: Function display_name takes raw: &str, fallback: &str and gives back String. :: signature :: 1899 :: 0
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: is_loopback_bind :: pub fn is_loopback_bind(host: &str) -> bool { :: True when `host` is a loopback listen address: 127.0.0.0/8, `::1`, or `localhost`.  `0.0.0.0` and `::` are not loopback — they accept remote connections. :: doc comment :: 2229 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: loopback_return :: pub fn loopback_return(raw: &str) -> Option<String> { :: Admit a desktop return URL: the address of the listener the app opened for this one sign-in attempt. The bar is the loopback literal itself, checked character by character — not a URL parse that a hostname or userinfo could slip through:  * scheme `http` exactly. Loopback has a real origin but no CA issues certificates for it; RFC 8252's native-app practice is plain http on the loopback interface, which never leaves the machine. * host the literal `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]` — not `localhost`, not any other name, not another loopback address. A name is whatever the resolver says it is (hosts files are editable), and the app binds the numeric loopback anyway. * an explicit port, 1–65535 — the app always listens on an ephemeral port it was given, never a default. * then only a path/query tail of plain URL characters: no `#` (a fragment never reaches a server anyway, and its presence means the value was not a listener address), no whitespace, controls, or non-ASCII.  The result is capped short enough to ride the sign-in bounce — this endpoint travels as the `next` of a `/?next=` redirect, well inside the length `safe_next` admits there. :: doc comment :: 1832 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: safe_next :: pub fn safe_next(next: &str) -> String { :: Keeps the post-sign-in redirect on this site: a path, optionally with a `#coordinate` fragment.  A `:` is refused in the path portion only. Past the `?`, a query may legitimately carry URLs of its own — an OAuth authorization request returning from sign-in names https redirect URIs and, for CIMD clients, an https client_id — and a colon there cannot move the destination off this site, because the value still begins with a single `/` and browsers resolve it as a path on this origin. The length cap admits those same authorization requests (a full `/oauth/authorize?...` round trip runs several hundred characters); the sealed pending cookie that carries `next` through a provider sign-in holds it comfortably. :: doc comment :: 1794 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: token_eq :: pub fn token_eq(expected: &str, given: &str) -> bool { :: Constant-time comparison; an empty expected value never matches. :: doc comment :: 1960 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: valid_state_nonce :: pub fn valid_state_nonce(state: &str) -> bool { :: The desktop attempt's state nonce: long enough to be unguessable, plain enough to travel in URLs and assertions unchanged. The app generates it (32 random hex/letters in practice); this only checks the shape. :: doc comment :: 1874 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: valid_who :: pub fn valid_who(who: &str) -> bool { :: Function valid_who takes who: &str and gives back bool. :: signature :: 1881 :: 0
