item+ function src/oauth.rs :: load_editors_file :: pub fn load_editors_file(&self, path: &std::path::Path) { :: Load additional editor entries from a file (one per line) and make it the backing file for future admissions. The phext-edit server calls this with its default admitted-editors file when `PHEXT_EDITORS_FILE` is not set. :: doc comment :: 1287 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: locked :: pub fn locked(&self) -> bool { :: True when some credential is required for the API. :: doc comment :: 1240 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: loopback_return :: pub fn loopback_return(raw: &str) -> Option<String> { :: Admit a desktop return URL: the address of the listener the app opened for this one sign-in attempt. The bar is the loopback literal itself, checked character by character — not a URL parse that a hostname or userinfo could slip through:  * scheme `http` exactly. Loopback has a real origin but no CA issues certificates for it; RFC 8252's native-app practice is plain http on the loopback interface, which never leaves the machine. * host the literal `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]` — not `localhost`, not any other name, not another loopback address. A name is whatever the resolver says it is (hosts files are editable), and the app binds the numeric loopback anyway. * an explicit port, 1–65535 — the app always listens on an ephemeral port it was given, never a default. * then only a path/query tail of plain URL characters: no `#` (a fragment never reaches a server anyway, and its presence means the value was not a listener address), no whitespace, controls, or non-ASCII.  The result is capped short enough to ride the sign-in bounce — this endpoint travels as the `next` of a `/?next=` redirect, well inside the length `safe_next` admits there. :: doc comment :: 1836 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: oauth :: pub fn oauth(&self) -> &Oauth { :: Function oauth takes no inputs beyond the value it is called on, and gives back &Oauth. :: signature :: 1244 :: 0
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: route :: pub fn route(&self, method: &str, path: &str, headers: &HashMap<String, String>) -> Option<Reply> { :: Handles `/api/session` and `/api/auth/*`. Returns None for other paths. :: doc comment :: 1316 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: safe_next :: pub fn safe_next(next: &str) -> String { :: Keeps the post-sign-in redirect on this site: a path, optionally with a `#coordinate` fragment.  A `:` is refused in the path portion only. Past the `?`, a query may legitimately carry URLs of its own — an OAuth authorization request returning from sign-in names https redirect URIs and, for CIMD clients, an https client_id — and a colon there cannot move the destination off this site, because the value still begins with a single `/` and browsers resolve it as a path on this origin. The length cap admits those same authorization requests (a full `/oauth/authorize?...` round trip runs several hundred characters); the sealed pending cookie that carries `next` through a provider sign-in holds it comfortably. :: doc comment :: 1798 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: security_header_block :: pub fn security_header_block(https: bool) -> String { :: Function security_header_block takes https: bool and gives back String. :: signature :: 1132 :: 0
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: security_header_block_csp :: pub fn security_header_block_csp(https: bool, csp_override: Option<&str>) -> String { :: Function security_header_block_csp takes https: bool, csp_override: Option<&str> and gives back String. :: signature :: 1114 :: 0
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: session :: pub fn session(&self, headers: &HashMap<String, String>) -> Option<Session> { :: The signed-in session for a request, if any. In `--local` mode this is the synthetic local developer (never for a forwarded request), mirroring `session_view`; otherwise it is the cookie session. :: doc comment :: 1255 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso :: pub fn sso( &self, aud: &str, headers: &HashMap<String, String>, query: &HashMap<String, String>, ) -> Reply { :: `GET /api/auth/sso/<aud>` — the cross-site handoff to one allowlisted audience (`mutable`, `exocortexia`, `wishnode`).  Layering follows the claims/submissions precedent in the servers: a signed-in account is enough; an editor's place is not required (`sso_admits` is the single predicate that decides). With a session, the reply 302s to the audience's callback with a freshly sealed assertion. Without one, it 302s to this site's own root with this endpoint as the `next`, so the ordinary provider sign-in returns the browser here and the handoff completes — the OAuth `next` itself stays same-site throughout. An audience outside the allowlist, or one not configured (no SSO secret, or no target for that audience), simply does not exist: 404 — checked before any session handling, so an unknown audience never enters the sign-in return flow. :: doc comment :: 1493 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: status_text :: pub fn status_text(status: u16) -> &'static str { :: Function status_text takes status: u16 and gives back &'static str. :: signature :: 1136 :: 0
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: token_eq :: pub fn token_eq(expected: &str, given: &str) -> bool { :: Constant-time comparison; an empty expected value never matches. :: doc comment :: 1964 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: valid_state_nonce :: pub fn valid_state_nonce(state: &str) -> bool { :: The desktop attempt's state nonce: long enough to be unguessable, plain enough to travel in URLs and assertions unchanged. The app generates it (32 random hex/letters in practice); this only checks the shape. :: doc comment :: 1878 :: 1
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: valid_who :: pub fn valid_who(who: &str) -> bool { :: Function valid_who takes who: &str and gives back bool. :: signature :: 1885 :: 0
