item+ function src/mmap.rs :: write_bytes :: pub fn write_bytes(&mut self, coord: Coordinate, payload: &[u8]) { :: Store `payload` at `coord` under the format's ingestion rule (libphext's `stored_form`, behind `insert_bytes`/`replace_bytes`): valid UTF-8 is stored as ordinary text; anything else becomes a binary scroll holding the exact bytes. Ingestion never rejects a payload. :: doc comment :: 505 :: 1
item+ function src/mmap.rs :: write_scroll :: pub fn write_scroll(&mut self, coord: Coordinate, content: String) -> bool { :: Write content to a scroll at the given coordinate. If the coordinate already exists (in mmap or overlay), it's modified. If it's new, it's inserted.  Binary-scroll guard: a well-formed binary scroll (an image, say) cannot be replaced through the text path — its stored form is a header plus base64, and a text edit would corrupt the payload silently. Returns false when the write is refused for that reason; the scroll is left exactly as it was. Writing the identical stored form back is a no-op success. Byte payloads go through `write_binary` / `write_bytes` instead. :: doc comment :: 461 :: 1
item+ function src/mmap.rs :: write_scroll_raw :: pub fn write_scroll_raw(&mut self, coord: Coordinate, content: String) { :: Store a scroll's stored form verbatim, with no binary guard. This is the byte path: callers pass either canonical binary scrolls (from `libphext::binary::encode_scroll`) or stored forms they have already validated. Prefer `write_scroll` for text and `write_binary` / `write_bytes` for payloads. :: doc comment :: 476 :: 1
item+ struct src/mmap.rs :: MappedLattice :: pub struct MappedLattice { :: A phext document with copy-on-write editing.  The document may be a chain (a multiverse): several member phexts — universes — in one file. All read/write methods operate on the *active* universe (see `universe` / `set_universe`); a plain phext is a chain of one universe and behaves exactly as it always has. :: doc comment :: 144 :: 1
